        "label": "Apply an amount across rows of another list",
        "job": "Apply the amount one row holds (a payment, a credit, a grant) across the open amounts of rows of another list (invoices, bills, budgets) as allocation rows, propose it first, take an allocation back, or read what is applied and open on a row",
        "description": "action propose works out, writing nothing, how the source row's amount would go across the targets in an order (oldest_first by default, or as read-data/list-settlements.json orders say) and what is left unapplied as a credit. apply keeps one allocation row per target (source, target, amount, date, request) in the allocations list \"<source>-to-<target>-allocations\"; give items [{ target_id, amount }] or leave them out to apply the proposal; request_key makes it apply once. Every item is answered done, already_done or refused with why, and the outcome says all, some or none. remove takes allocation_ids back, reopening exactly those targets. position reads one row ({ row: { row_id, amount_field }, role, allocations: { list_id } }): its amount, applied, open and the allocations behind it. A row with no amount is refused: empty is not zero. Body: source { row_id, amount_field }, targets { list_id, amount_field, order_field?, row_ids? }, order, allocations { list_id?, *_field? }.",
        "input_schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "required": [
            "action",
            "list_id",
            "source_kind",
            "target_kind"
          ],
          "properties": {
            "action": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "propose",
                "apply",
                "remove",
                "position"
              ]
            },
            "list_id": {
              "type": "string",
              "minLength": 1,
              "description": "The list in the request: the source (or the lines, or the row's list) when the body leaves its own list_id out."
            },
